Itsumi Saito, Keisuke Sakaguchi, Kentaro Inui\n\nLarge vision-language models (LVLMs) demonstrate strong performance on diagram understanding benchmarks, yet they still struggle with understanding relationships between elements, particularly those represented by nodes and directed edges (e.g., arrows and lines). To investigate the underlying causes of this limitation, we probe the internal representation of LVLMs using a carefully constructed synthetic diagram dataset based on directed graphs. Our probing experiments reveal that edge information is not linearly separable in the vision encoder and becomes linearly encoded only in the text tokens in the language model. In contrast, node information and global structural features are already linearly encoded in individual hidden states of the vision encoder. These findings suggest that the stage at which linearly separable representations are formed varies depending on the type of visual information. In particular, the delayed emergence of edge representations may help explain why LVLMs struggle with relational understanding, such as interpreting edge directions, which require more abstract, compositionally integrated processes.\n\nSubjects: Computation and Language , Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\n\nPublish: 2026-03-03 11:17:31 UTC\n\n#1 Nodes Are Early, Edges Are Late: Probing Diagram Representations in Large Vision-Language Models\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) CoDAR: Continuous Diffusion Language Models are More Powerful Than You Think (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/401523353_CoDAR_Continuous_Diffusion_Language_Models_are_More_Powerful_Than_You_Think)\nciteturn32106search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; # CoDAR: Continuous Diffusion Language Models are More Powerful Than You Think  * March 2026 ... Junzhe Shen\n\n# CoDAR: Continuous Diffusion Language Models are More Powerful Than You Think\n\n  * March 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2603.02547\n\nAuthors:\n\nJunzhe Shen\n\nJunzhe Shen\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nJieru Zhao\n\nJieru Zhao\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nZiwei He\n\nZiwei He\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nZhouhan Lin\n\nZhouhan Lin\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nWe study why continuous diffusion language models (DLMs) have lagged behind discrete diffusion approaches despite their appealing continuous generative dynamics. Under a controlled token--recovery study, we identify token rounding, the final projection from denoised embeddings to tokens, as a primary bottleneck. Building on these insights, we propose CoDAR (Continuous Diffusion with Contextual AutoRegressive Decoder), a two--stage framework that keeps diffusion entirely continuous in an embedding space while learning a strong, context--conditional discretizer: an autoregressive Transformer decoder that cross--attends to the denoised embedding sequence and performs contextualized rounding to tokens. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome skins not carrying over after merging PS4 Overwatch account with PC - Technical Support - Overwatch Forums (https://eu.forums.blizzard.com/en/overwatch/t/some-skins-not-carrying-over-after-merging-ps4-overwatch-account-with-pc/38856)\nciteturn32106search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; # Some skins not carrying over after merging PS4 Overwatch account with PC ... ace-212626 March 8, 2026, 8:02pm 1 ... SwedishCabe-2652 March 9, 2026, 4:34pm 2I’d take a look at the dedicated FAQ released leading up to the cross-progression merger.\n\n# Some skins not carrying over after merging PS4 Overwatch account with PC\n\nTechnical Support\n\nace-212626 March 8, 2026, 8:02pm 1\n\nHi everyone,\n\nI’m wondering if anyone else has experienced something similar or knows if this is something that can be checked.\n\nI originally played Overwatch on PS4 years ago and later merged that account with my PC account when I switched platforms. At the time I assumed everything transferred correctly, but recently I started thinking about some skins and cosmetics that I clearly remember having on my PlayStation account that don’t seem to be on my current account.\n\nThis merge happened a long time ago, so I’m not sure if it’s even possible to recover or verify anything now. But I’m curious if:\n\n  * anyone else has had missing cosmetics after merging accounts\n\n  * there’s a way to check account history or old inventories\n\n  * Blizzard Entertainment support can still look into something like this after so many years\n\nI’ve already contacted support, but I thought I’d ask here as well in case someone has had the same issue.\n\nThanks!\n\nSwedishCabe-2652 March 9, 2026, 4:34pm 2\n\nI’d take a look at the dedicated FAQ released leading up to the cross-progression merger.\n\nAs it mentions, a big emphasis was put on ensuring the right accounts were merged.\n\nIf all the steps within it were followed and all the criteria met, I hope support can help.\n\n* * *\n\n> ## Cross-progression is coming to Overwatch 2: Account merge FAQ\n> \n> Blizzard Entertainment | August 16, 2022\n> \n> With the launch of cross-progression, players can merge multiple Overwatch accounts to carry progression and in-game cosmetics into Overwatch 2 on a single profile. Starting August 16, every player with a linked console account will be prompted at login to identify, select, and confirm the accounts you want to merge. Once you approve, we will prepare your accounts to be merged during the release of Overwatch 2.\n\n> | `https://overwatch.blizzard.com/en-us/news/23824001/cross-progression-is-coming-to-overwatch-2-account-merge-faq/`----------------------------------